    "title": "PROFESSOR definition in American English | Collins English Dictionary",
    "text": "professor (prəfɛsər ) Word forms: plural professors 1. countable noun & title noun & vocative noun A professor in an American or Canadian university or college is a teacher of the highest rank. ...a professor of economics at George Washington University. 2. title noun & countable noun & vocative noun A professor in a British university is the most senior teacher in a department who has been appointed to the position due to their academic achievements. ...Professor Cameron. Collins COBUILD Advanced Learner’s Dictionary. Copyright © HarperCollins Publishers American English pronunciation ! It seems that your browser is blocking this video content.To access it, add this site to the exceptions or modify your security settings, then refresh this page. British English pronunciation ! It seems that your browser is blocking this video content.To access it, add this site to the exceptions or modify your security settings, then refresh this page. professor in American English (proʊˈfɛsər , prəˈfɛsər ) nounOrigin: ME professoure < L, teacher < professus: see profess 2. a. a college or university teacher of the highest academic rank; full professor c. loosely any college, university, or, occas., secondary-school teacher Webster’s New World College Dictionary, 5th Digital Edition. Copyright © 2025 HarperCollins Publishers. Derived forms professorial (ˌproʊfəˈsɔriəl , ˌprɑfəˈsɔriəl )adjective professorially (ˌprofesˈsorially) adverb professorship (proˈfessorˌship) or professorate (proʊˈfɛsərɪt , prəˈfɛsərɪt )noun professor in American English (prəˈfesər) noun1. a teacher of the highest academic rank in a college or university, who has been awarded the title Professor in a particular branch of learning; a full professor a professor of Spanish literature 4. an instructor in some art or skilled sport a professor of singing a professor of boxing 5. a person who professes his or her sentiments, beliefs, etc Most material © 2005, 1997, 1991 by Penguin Random House LLC. Modified entries © 2019 by Penguin Random House LLC and HarperCollins Publishers Ltd Derived formsprofessorial (ˌproufəˈsɔriəl, -ˈsour-, ˌprɑfə-)adjective Word origin [1350–1400; ME ‹ ML prōfessor one who has taken the vows of a religious order, L: a public lecturer, equiv. to prō- pro-1 + -fet-, comb. form of fatērī to acknowledge, declare + -tor -tor, with tt ˃ ss] professor in British English (prəˈfɛsə ) Collins English Dictionary. Copyright © HarperCollins Publishers Derived formsprofessorial (ˌprɒfɪˈsɔːrɪəl )adjective professorially (ˌprofesˈsorially)adverb Word originC14: from Medieval Latin: one who has made his profession in a religious order, from Latin: a public teacher; see profess Quotations A professor is one who talks in someone else's sleep Word lists with professor educationQuick word challengeQuiz ReviewQuestion: 1 - Score: 0 / 5 Related word partners professor",
